course: dictionary
lesson: 11047
title: What is a website?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A website is a group of pages on the internet that belong to one person, company or organisation. You visit it by typing its web address. «Websites are where you can look things up, play learning games and read the news.»
explain: Your device does not keep the whole website. «A website is stored on a computer called a server, and your device asks the server for it.» The server sends the pages back, and your browser shows them to you as a page.
explain: Being on a website does not make something true. «Not everything on a website is true.» When you read a website, ask who wrote it, how they know, and whether a trusted place says the same. Check facts in more than one place.
fact: A website is a group of pages on the internet.
fact: A website belongs to one person, company or organisation.
fact: You visit a website using its web address, such as www.example.com.
fact: «A website is a set of files stored on a server and sent to your browser, which shows them as a page.»
fact: The World Wide Web was invented by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989.
exercise: What is a website? | *A group of pages on the internet that belong to one person, company or organisation | A message you send to a friend in a game | A machine you plug into a wall | A paper book about computers
exercise: Where is a website kept before you see it? | *On a computer called a server | Inside your password | In your school bag | In the back of a library book
exercise: Which way is the best way to check something you read on a website? | Share it straight away with your friends | *Check it in more than one place, and ask a trusted adult | Believe it, because it is on a website | Ask a stranger online
test: Why should you check facts you see on a website? | Because websites are always wrong | *Because not everything on a website is true | Because only teachers are allowed to read websites | Because websites cannot show pictures
test: «Real game companies never ask for your password on another website or in chat.» A chat message asks for your password. What should you do? | Send it to a friend to check | Post it on a website so everyone can see | Type it in quickly, in case it is a real company | *Do not give it, and tell a trusted adult
exam: «Handing in work you did not do, from a person, a website or an AI chatbot, is cheating.» What does this sentence tell you? | Using a website to learn is always cheating | Websites are the best place to hand in your work | Chatbots are allowed in every test | *Copying work from a website is cheating, just like copying from a person
